Z||000000||Swed71||11-09-2001||07:59 AM||||I have a 85 Manual V6 and I have the following problem:<BR>A few weeks ago I let my girlfriend drive the car for a few minutes because she just wanted to test it (she don't have a drivers license), after a while she drove really slow on the second gear and it stalled and dies, when I then started the car and drove away I noticed that it was very weak and did not sound right.<BR>It's really weak and I can't get it to run faster than 30 Mph on third gear, if I put in fourth gear it will slowly run slower because it's to weak...<BR>I also noticed that the manifold will start to glow after a while...<P>I know that it's not the head gasket...<P><BR>Thnx  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000001||Oreif||11-09-2001||08:13 AM||||The catalytic converter could be clogged. Does the exhaust smell like it's running very rich? <BR>A big vacuum leak. Check the EGR tube where it attaches to the intake plenum. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Swed71||11-09-2001||08:22 AM||||No the exhaust do not smell unusual..<P>I suspected a vacuum leak as well, but I have checked all the vacuum hoses, I haven't checked the EGR tube thoroughly though (just made sure that the hoses where properly connected).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||11-09-2001||09:06 AM||||It is acting exactly like my car did when it's converter plugged up.  Check  your temperature, too.  Mine started running hotter and hotter as it got worse.  It will continue to get worse until the car is undriveable, if it is the converter.||||reg||4
Z||000004||Philphine||11-09-2001||11:08 AM||||maybe check for a leak at a vacume line where part of it is attached to the fire wall or some non moving area in the engine compartment. i've had that happen on a couple of cars. maybe when she was driving and it stalled the engine moved more than usual and it broke/cracked/pulled away from a connection that is stationary and you haven't noticed it yet.||||reg||1
Z||000005||johnt671||11-09-2001||08:11 PM||||If the car idles ok but then loses power under load it is probibly the cat.  I've had that happen on a couple of cars.  It would start and run but then when I drove them there was no power.  Another thing that might cause this is an old fuel filter or some kind of fuel pressure problem.  If your fuel filter is old it might be worth changing it and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero_nightmare||11-10-2001||02:51 AM||||check for computer codes,when i first got the 86 camaro (2.8) i used to have it had the EXACT same problem and it turned out to be the throttle position sensor (TPS).   <P>------------------<BR> <P>85 GT pics  <A HREF="http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw" TARGET=_blank>http://communities.msn.com/russcars/85fierogt.msnw</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fiero_nightmare (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Swed71||11-10-2001||09:25 AM||||the car do not idle ok, it actually runs as ****  all the time, if I don't keep the gas pedal pressed down it will stall and die as well....<P>I just realized that some (one or two) of the ignition cables (I think that's the right word  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) may be broken, I will replace them all today to see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Swed71||11-12-2001||03:15 AM||||I think that it might be the EGR tube that is broken, where is it located and how large is it and can I just remove it without replacing it?<P>I'm not so familiar with engines (I know the Common stuff).....<P>Thnx for all the answers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||11-12-2001||06:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Swed71:<BR><B>I think that it might be the EGR tube that is broken, where is it located and how large is it and can I just remove it without replacing it?<P>I'm not so familiar with engines (I know the Common stuff).....<P>Thnx for all the answers   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The EGR Tube is connected to the EGR Valve. The EGR valve is mounted on the 'Y' pipe or crossover pipe and looks like a mushroom. The EGR Tube is connected to it with two nuts and is as big around as your index finger. The Tube connects to the bottom of the upper plenum just behind the throttle body. The usually break at the EGR Valve and it will be just a crack if new.<BR>Have you checked the EGR valve for proper operation?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Swed71||11-12-2001||07:56 AM||||Thnx for the description, I think I know where I should look now..<P>No, I haven't checked anything so far (except for the plug wires), I blew the head gasket 6 month ago, and at the same time they replaced the map sensor and the Throttle Position Sensor (I don't know id that matters) they also replaced the EGR Tube at the same time....<P>As I mentioned before, I have a very limited knowledge of engines, but I can do some simpler testing if someone just tells me how to do it...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroluke||11-12-2001||08:03 AM||||Hi,<P>first, check if you have no trouble codes stored. A bad MAP or TPS sensor may cause weird driveability problems, because it is used by the ECM as the main source of information. If you have access to a scantool, check the readings, because a bad sensor does not necessarily set a trouble code!<P>Next, your catalytic converter is on the suspect list, as others have pointed out.<P>I don't think a bad EGR-tube is the problem, because if it cracks, you get more air and high idle, but not lack of power.<P>Check if the distributor is tight, because if it is not, it may have rotated and your timing is off. Checking your timing is a good  idea anyway.<P>If you can, check fuel pressure, because a bad regulator or fuel pump also results in lack of power. A cheap alternative test is<BR>"start pilot" or similar spray (careful, it's  explosive!). If the engine idles fine while <BR>you spray this stuff into the intake (take the air cleaner cover off), you have a fuel problem.<P>If it's not fuel related, and your timing is right, check the plugs, but I double all 6 of<BR>them got bad at the same time. <P>If your EGR-Valve is bad (open all the time), your engine will also run like crap. Check if it only opens when you apply vacuum to the valve.<P>If all that works, it's gotta pretty much be the cat. If it's older than 5 years, it won't hurt to replace it anyway.<P>Just my $0.02<P><BR>Best regards,<P>-Oliver||||reg||1
Z||000012||gt boy||11-12-2001||08:55 PM||||i am sorry that i can not help you with your problem...but i just wanted to say ...welcome to the forum!<BR><P>------------------<BR>"it's too bad he had to run out of talent half way through the corner..."||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fierobruiser||11-13-2001||01:47 AM||||My 88 Duke with 90K on the clock had the same symptoms a couple of months ago. It turned out that one of the coils had gone bad and had taken out the ignition control module. $200.00 at the Fiero Store and all was right with the world again. <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
